TY  - JOUR
TI  - Board Gender Diversity and Corporate Citizenship Actions, Reporting and Reputation
AU  - Viviers, Suzette
AU  - Mans-Kemp, Nadia
AB  - [Researchers in developed markets have established that board gender diversity could promote good corporate citizenship. South Africa presents a unique emerging market setting given its history of gender inequality and escalating social and environmental challenges. In this positivistic study, a context-specific dataset was constructed to measure corporate citizenship actions, reporting and reputation among a sample of companies listed on the Johannesburg Stock Exchange (JSE) over the period 2009 to 2015. A significant positive relationship was noted between board gender diversity and selected corporate citizenship actions, namely the presence of an environmental quality management policy, waste reduced, waste recycled and employee corporate social responsibility training. A significant positive relationship was also observed between female board representation and corporate citizenship reputation, measured in terms of a company's inclusion in the FTSE/JSE Responsible Investment index. These findings could be attributed to women bringing different perspectives on stakeholder management to local boardrooms. Although the number of female directors has increased over the research period, much remains to be done to enhance board gender diversity. In the absence of mandatory gender quotas, local companies should do more to create opportunities for the training, development and support of female board candidates.]
